nvk-nak-occ-target: a heavy graphics shader is built again under a lower register cap when it gains warps (3.0.15).
NullMoth's own change (not an upstream Mesa commit).

Phase 1 of the compiler work. The register file of an SM holds 65536 registers, so a shader's register count sets how
many warps an SM can keep in flight (max_warps_per_sm: GPRs in steps of 8, warps in steps of 4); a fragment shader
at 104 registers runs 16 warps per SM, at 80 it runs 24. A shader that waits on textures or global memory hides that
wait with other warps, so fewer registers can be worth a few more instructions.

For a vertex, tessellation, geometry or fragment shader that ends below 32 warps per SM, samples a texture or reads
global memory, and has no register budget of its own (NVMTL's RAW budget on SM 120), nak_compile_shader_internal builds
it again with assign_regs capped at the largest register count that gains warps (the cap goes through the existing
NVMTL_RA_BUDGET hook, never below the hardware-reserved registers plus 32), and keeps the new build only when it really
fits under the cap, really gains warps, and has no more than 5 % more instructions than the first build. It tries at
most twice, a step lower each time, and a build that panics is dropped. Compute shaders and shaders at 32 warps or more
are never rebuilt, and nothing is rebuilt in WindowServer: it has no shell environment and NAK does not read
nvrm610.conf, so the way back below could not reach it, and there the compiler stays as it was in 3.0.14.

On by default. NAK_OCC_TARGET=0 (read once per process) keeps the first build always, which is the compiler of 3.0.14
to the instruction; the switch is in the shader cache key table of nvk-nak-switch-key, so binaries built under one
setting are not served under the other.
